    "c1": {
      "anchor": "costs#markdown-1",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "13. What this gives up",
      "excerpt": "A start with no setup: joining takes an AT Protocol account, which most people d",
      "text": "Is it possible to change this, so my posts are written by me, but the org can stop me if I'm trying to do something I'm not allowed to do?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T22:13:47.995Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Owning what you wrote in a group: it stays with the org when you leave, and other apps see the org as its author.",
      "offset": 190,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T22:14:20.965Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T23:37:10.458Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, it's possible, and how you described it is now in \"How it maps to the protocol\". Spell checks first, then writes to your repo. With an external account you keep your posts. With an account made in the org, they stay with the org unless it lets you take them.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T22:23:49.333Z"
      }
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "costs#markdown-1",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "13. What this gives up",
      "excerpt": "A start with no setup: joining takes an AT Protocol account, which most people d",
      "text": "Hmm, these could be purged?  e.g. versions only held for 30 days?  Also, who has access to these git accounts?  Presumably admins...\n\nWhat if, instead of git, there was a lightweight versioning scheme in the admin space.  Here's a scenario:\n\nI want to create a file in a project.  Is start typing as an unnamed draft.  \n\nAt some point I say \"yah, let's get feedback on this\", I \"publish\" the file and name it \"Thoughts.md\".  Now other people can see it.  A version is saved in the admin \"backups\" space as `Thoughts.<date>.md`.\n\nI want to make changes, so I say \"new draft.\"  I hack on the draft.  I want to re-publish.  This replaces main \"Thoughts\" doc.  `Thoughts.<newdate.md>` is saved in the admin space.\n\nI decide I want to go back to the original, I can see the versions of the file in the admin space and restore (or just copy out bits I wanted to get back).\n\nRetention on these backups can be an org-level policy.  So all but the current version (or last 2, etc) could be purged after 30 days.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T22:14:53.517Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Spell keeps the git history of every file, private ones included",
      "offset": 386,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T22:21:12.983Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T23:37:11.424Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, and this is better than git: no second store, so no read gate of Spell's, and deleting is real. It's now \"Files, drafts and versions\", with drafts in your inbox and retention as an org policy in the Admin space. As you chose, versions sit in the project's private space rather than the Admin space, so its Leads can restore them.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T22:23:50.269Z"
      }
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "costs#markdown-1",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "13. What this gives up",
      "excerpt": "A start with no setup: joining takes an AT Protocol account, which most people d",
      "text": "Don't understand what you mean",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T22:21:32.647Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Plain files for rows: saved",
      "offset": 452,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T23:37:12.391Z",
          "html": "<p>My wording was poor. I meant: an app's saved rows are records on the host, not files you can browse, so a folder of plain JSON is an export Spell has to write. Reworded that way.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T22:23:51.185Z"
      }
    },
    "c4": {
      "anchor": "costs#markdown-1",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "13. What this gives up",
      "excerpt": "A start with no setup: joining takes an AT Protocol account, which most people d",
      "text": "Use `ATSpace` here please if that's what you mean.\n\nAssuming we had, e.g. a desktop app, I should be able to have a local copy of the space that I can work on while on the plane. ?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T22:22:04.731Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Offline: a Space can't work offline, so a data folder stays the local copy",
      "offset": 717,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T22:22:30.682Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T23:37:13.352Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes to both. The doc now says ATSpace wherever it means AT Protocol's. And a desktop or phone app keeps a local copy you can read and edit on a plane: your changes wait until you're back online, when the write server checks them and may refuse one.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T22:23:52.117Z"
      }
    }
  },
